    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" class="grid" style="margin: 0;">
                <tr id="comment-header-10001"><td bgcolor="#f0f0f0">
            Comment by  
                                                    
                
        
            <a class="user-hover" rel="712020:3b8a3cbb-da59-490f-bf68-8e04925d3bc3"
                id="word_commented_712020:3b8a3cbb-da59-490f-bf68-8e04925d3bc3"
                href="https://laveshs051.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=712020%3A3b8a3cbb-da59-490f-bf68-8e04925d3bc3">
                    Lavesh Sawant
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">07/Jul/26</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-10001"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p>Incident Report:(192.168.189.128) outbound to 192.168.189.129 at <span class="error">&#91;Time&#93;</span>. High-velocity application protocol anomaly identified. Detected by Splunk (EventCode 3), Wazuh (Rule 60122), and network flow mapping. Abnormal network sockets opened on non-standard port 4444.<br/>
Active Response: Traffic Blocked. Severity: HIGH.</p>

<p>Time of Activity: <tt>2026-07-06</tt> at 11:32</p>

<p>Reason for classifying as true positive: Analysis of Splunk EventCode 3 shows a rapid, programmatic spike of 10 sequential outbound network connection attempts originating from native windows binary 'curl.exe' targeting the external host 192.168.189.129 on port 4444 within a 5-second window. The high connection velocity and destination port choice confirm a data transmission or exfiltration loop simulation.</p>

<p>Reason for escalating the alert: Found automated data transfer signatures matching telemetry parameters for unauthorized information staging. The attack maps perfectly to the MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework technique T1071 (Application Layer Protocol) utilizing Sysmon Event Code 3 for explicit socket validation.</p>

<p>Recommended Remediation Actions: Implement an immediate outbound firewall block rule on port 4444, blacklist the destination network space, and review parent process trees to verify if the curl action was spawned by a compromised web service or script file.</p>
        </td></tr>
            </table>
